      "title": "How a Truss Bridge Carries Load",
      "points": [
        {
          "point": "A truss is a frame of triangles, and triangles stay rigid because they cannot change shape without a side changing length, unlike a rectangle that can lean into a parallelogram",
          "chips": [
            "1. What a truss is"
          ]
        },
        {
          "point": "Every truss member carries force along its length, either tension pulling it or compression pushing it",
          "chips": [
            "2. Tension and compression"
          ]
        },
        {
          "point": "In a simple bridge truss the top chord is in compression, the bottom chord is in tension, and diagonals alternate between the two",
          "chips": [
            "2. Tension and compression"
          ]
        },
        {
          "point": "The load path runs from the vehicle's weight on the deck, to the truss joints, from member to member toward the ends",
          "chips": [
            "3. The load path"
          ]
        },
        {
          "point": "Supports at each end push up on the truss and pass the load into the ground, completing the load path",
          "chips": [
            "3. The load path"
          ]
        }
